{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/actor/480","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"last_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/last_name"}],"content":"Hamilton Beach","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"first_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/first_name"}],"content":"","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"Hamilton Beach","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"biography statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435422"}],"content":"Hamilton Beach Brands Holding Company is an American designer, marketer and distributor of home appliances and commercial restaurant equipment marketed primarily in the United States, Canada, and Mexico, including blenders, mixers, toasters, slow cookers, clothes irons, and air purifiers. \n \nUntil sometime in the 1980s the company's products were marketed under the brand name \"Hamilton Beach Scovill\", reflecting a merger that occurred in 1923. In 1990, the company merged with Proctor Silex, another household appliance manufacturer. Key market competitors include Cuisinart, Black \u0026 Decker,  Salton, De'Longhi, and Sunbeam. (Sourced from Wikipedia May 2023)","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Actor"}